WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.291
In a move that could throw the release of
Fast and Furious 9 into a tailspin, AMC Theatres

00:00:05.291 --> 00:00:09.820
says its plans to release any Universal-backed
films are coming to a screeching halt after

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.590
the studio announced its record-breaking success
with Trolls World Tour.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.960
The head of the American movie theater chain
sent NBCUniversal a blistering message after

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.590
comments from its CEO Jeff Shell implied the
Hollywood studio might be moving forward with

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.440
a dual theatrical and at-home release model.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.260
Adam Aron, AMC Theatres' chair-CEO, said in
a letter to the studio:

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.340
"It is disappointing to us, but Jeff's comments
as to Universal's unilateral actions and intentions

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.000
have left us with no choice.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.960
Therefore, effectively immediately AMC will
no longer play any Universal movies in any

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.620
of our theaters in the United States, Europe
or the Middle East."

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.190
Nearly all of AMC's 11,000 screens across
the globe have gone black, as the world's

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.780
largest theater circuit was forced to close
locations indefinitely following government-imposed

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.760
limits on social gatherings amid the coronavirus
pandemic.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.970
While some states are lifting their restrictions
in the coming weeks, the nation-wide closures

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.330
in March caused studios to push their spring
release slate to late summer at the earliest,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.120
leaving theater owners with nothing to screen
and little reason to reopen.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.120
But Aron's statement assures that even when
they do, the decades-long business relationship

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.020
between AMC Theatres and NBCUniversal will
have, quote, "sadly come to an end."

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.030
"You don't turn your back on family."

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.700
Originally geared up for a May 9 theatrical
debut, Universal tentpole F9 is among the

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.640
growing list of titles delayed due to the
ongoing pandemic.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.000
Universal pushed the ninth chapter of its
high-octane Fast and Furious franchise by

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.090
a whole year to April 2, 2021.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.909
The shift followed earlier comments from star
Vin Diesel promising fans that the May 2020

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:45.590
release of F9, which will see the return of
fan-favorite character Han, was still on.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:50.070
Unlike its plan with F9, Universal made the
huge decision amid the coronavirus pandemic

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.990
to ditch a theatrical release window for Trolls
World Tour in favor of a premium video on

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.340
demand debut.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.760
It was unclear how successful the gamble would
be, but the animated sequel reportedly exceeded

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:06.400
NBCUniversal's expectations, earning an estimated
$100 million in the first three weeks of its

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.450
North American VOD launch.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.009
Shell told The Wall Street Journal:

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:14.200
"As soon as theaters reopen, we expect to
release movies on both formats."

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.340
Neither AMC nor NATO, the National Association
of Theatre Owners, responded well to Shell's

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:20.480
comment.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.470
After declaring that the policy announcement
was, quote, "not some hollow or ill-considered

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.870
threat," Aron added that it would apply to
anyone considering expanding into a premium

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.490
video on demand model.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.090
"Not this time, Dom."

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:37.230
A speedy and pointed response from Universal
clarified that Shell's comments on release

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.200
window and platform plans had been misunderstood.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.120
According to the studio statement, theatrical
releases were never off the table, with NBCUniversal

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.400
only planning to do, quote, "PVOD when that
distribution outlet makes sense."

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.620
The statement reads in part:

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.960
"We look forward to having additional private
conversations with our exhibition partners,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.319
but are disappointed by this seemingly coordinated
attempt from AMC and NATO to confuse our position

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:01.860
and our actions."

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.810
If an agreement between AMC and Universal
can't be met between now and when theaters

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.940
do reopen, fans may never get to see F9 race
into theaters.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.080
In its own follow-up statement, the National
Association of Theater Owners challenged Universal's

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.290
characterization of theater owners' response
as, quote, "a coordinated attempt" at misrepresenting

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.590
Shell's quote.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:24.420
Patrick Corcoran, the group's vice president
and CCO, said:

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.070
"Unfortunately, Universal has a destructive
tendency to both announce decisions affecting

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:31.940
their exhibitor partners without actually
consulting with those partners, and now of

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.989
making unfounded accusations."

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:38.500
The entire debacle, which took place mostly
over a day, highlighted an increasingly tense

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.630
relationship between theater owners and movie
studios.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:46.319
Nearly a decade after rental and streaming
services practically ended the DVD market,

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:50.750
theater owners are facing declining ticket
sales and an expensive ongoing effort to modernize

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.580
various elements of the theater-going experience.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.300
Following the closures in March, some Wall
Street analysts have even predicted AMC might

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.310
be forced to file for Chapter 11 bankruptcy.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:04.510
Despite its woes, a world where the fantastical
action and drama of Dom Toretto's crew never

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.120
grace the big screen seems incomprehensible.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.000
For some, so too is the idea that Universal's
success with Trolls World Tour's PVOD release

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.050
is sustainable.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.799
The movie's $100 million earnings are notable
because NBCUniversal was able to achieve that

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:23.150
by charging at-home viewers $19.99 not to
buy the film, but merely to rent it for two

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.150
days.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.289
But as NATO president and CEO John Fithian
said in the organization's response to the

00:04:28.289 --> 00:04:33.589
studio, it achieved this during, quote, "unusual
circumstances in an unprecedented environment."

00:04:33.589 --> 00:04:34.809
He explained:

00:04:34.809 --> 00:04:39.699
"Theaters provide a beloved immersive, shared
experience that cannot be replicated an experience

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:43.410
that many of the VOD viewers of this film
would have participated in had the world not

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.580
been sequestered at home, desperate for something
new to watch with their families."

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.229
It's unclear how this will all turn out, but
for the sake of Fast and Furious fans, let's

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:54.490
hope it all blows over in time for the 2021
premiere.

